</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Hongxia Yang<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">†</span></sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Department of Computing, University of Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Hong Kong, China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>InfiX.ai
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author"><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Correspondence to: </span><a href="mailto:hongxia.yang@polyu.edu.hk">hongxia.yang@polyu.edu.hk</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown substantial advances through reinforcement learning (RL), particularly in domains where rewards can be programmatically verified, such as mathematics and code. In these areas, models benefit from a well-defined operational base guided by explicit rule-based objectives.
However, this progress reveals a significant limitation: in open-ended domains where rewards are ambiguous, subjective, or context-dependent, such as creative writing, scientific reasoning, and notably medical consultation, robust reward functions are lacking, making these areas challenging for current RL strategies.
To bridge this gap, we introduce <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ORBIT</span>, an <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">o</span>pen-ended <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">r</span>ubric-<span id="abstract1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">b</span>ased <span id="abstract1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">i</span>ncremental <span id="abstract1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">t</span>raining framework specifically designed for high-stakes medical dialogue.
ORBIT integrates synthetic dialogue generation with the dynamic creation of rubrics, employing these rubrics to direct an incremental RL process.
In particular, this approach does not depend on external medical knowledge or manual rules, instead utilizing rubric-guided feedback to shape learning.
When implemented on the Qwen3-4B-Instruct model, our method can greatly enhance its performance on the HealthBench-Hard benchmark from <span id="abstract1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">7.0 to 27.2 using only 2k samples</span>, thus achieving state-of-the-art results for models of this scale.
Our analysis confirms that rubric-driven RL fosters consistent performance gains across diverse consultation scenarios, going beyond simple numerical improvements.
These findings underscore rubric-based feedback as a scalable strategy for advancing LLMs in intricate, open-ended tasks.
Code is available at <a href="https://github.com/pidneuralode/ORBIT" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/pidneuralode/ORBIT</a><span id="abstract1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.</span></p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Machine Learning, ICML
</div><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_affiliationnotice"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">affiliationnotice: </span>Equal contribution</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In recent years, the significance of the post-training phase in the development of Large Language Models (LLMs) has grown substantially, a trend underscored by the emergence of models like DeepSeek-Math <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and a corresponding body of literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
A prevailing finding in this research is that Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) is particularly effective for knowledge memorization, while Reinforcement Learning (RL) excels at enhancing generalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shenfeld et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Through the use of a comprehensive and diverse SFT corpus, a model can acquire domain-specific knowledge and, simultaneously, adapt rapidly to the instructional formats of downstream tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
The subsequent RL phase, building upon this strong foundation, then assists the model in exploring the boundaries of its capabilities through reward signals based on interactions with a given corpus.
As a result of extensive practical application, the SFT+RL sequence has become the predominant post-training paradigm, although alternative approaches, such as ’zero-RL’ methods  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, also exist.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">RLVR(Reinforcement with Verifiable Rewards) shows promising enhancement on tasks like Math, Code, and other STEM subjects with verifiable format. Despite the quick development of scientific-related reinforcement learning, we have many open-ended subjects in real world scenarios like medicine, psychology, and sociology. Benchmarks which adopt rubrics as assessment criterion are proved to be useful to evaluate the both ”personality” and professional level of LLMs. Among open-ended benchmarks, Healthbench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arora et al., 2025</a>)</cite> pave the way for evaluating the medical ability of LLMs with expert-designed hand-written rubrics for special clinical cases. Many medical expert-level medical QA models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> can only get zero points on HealthBench Hard, indicating the huge gap between QA benchmarks and open-ended benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To enhance the open-ended medical ability of LLMs, we propose ORBIT, a post-training system that can greatly enhance the performance of LLMs on HealthBench. We build an automatic rubrics generation strategy without finetuning a LLM or requiring human efforts. We first adopt a RAG module to recall the top query related to a specific clinical query we collected and utilize the in-context learning ability of LLMs to generate corresponding rubrics. We have systematically reviewed the selection of LLMs for the best rubric design. In order to improve both efficiency and performance, we carefully design sample-level and rubric-level selection module. Finally, we conduct a comprehensive ablation on variables among the RL training process including rubric design, judge model selection, and data curation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our contributions include:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A fully automated rubrics generation paradigm, which can be potentially useful in enhancing performances of various open-ended evaluation benchmarks.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A systematic verification of strategies to filter data from both sample-level and rubric-level.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A comprehensive ablation study on components of the ORBIT system to optimize the performance of the rubrics-based RL training paradigm.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Open-Ended Benchmarks</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The evaluation of large language models (LLMs) on open-ended generation tasks is undergoing a paradigm shift, moving from conventional automated metrics toward more holistic, rubric-based frameworks.
Early benchmarks were often limited to short-form text grading, fixed entity extraction, or weakly correlated metrics from natural language generation.
These methods proved insufficient for capturing the sophisticated, multi-faceted capabilities of modern LLMs.
This limitation has spurred the development of a new generation of evaluation suites that employ multi-dimensional, fine-grained rubrics.
Prominent examples include HealthBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arora et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, VISTA (Scale AI, 2025), PaperBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Starace et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, WildBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, AMEGA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fast et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and MultiChallenge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deshpande et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
By systematically defining thousands of evaluation criteria across diverse scenarios, these benchmarks enable a more precise and comprehensive assessment of model behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">HealthBench, in particular, demonstrates how large-scale, rubric-based evaluation can delineate performance boundaries in the medical consultation domain, a task that remains a significant challenge for current models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Rubric-based RL in LLM</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The role of Reinforcement Learning (RL) in the post-training of Large Language Models (LLMs) has become increasingly critical.
Early methodologies, such as Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, mainly relied on holistic feedback from preference data to assess the overall quality of a model’s response.
However, this approach often provides only a coarse-grained evaluation.
Subsequently, a shift occurred towards more granular rule-based RL techniques.
These methods began by rewarding specific structured components of an answer and later evolved to incorporate rewards for adherence to particular output formats <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Zhang, 2024</a>)</cite>.
This was followed by the development of RL methods centered on even more fine-grained semantic reward signals, as exemplified by recent work in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhaskar et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jayalath et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Viswanathan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
This trend has also been reflected in the medical domain, with several specialized approaches emerging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gunjal et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Despite this progress, significant challenges remain. Key open questions include: how to construct appropriate and scalable rubric data for reward modeling; how to design effective and reproducible pipelines; and how to make these resources open-source to foster broader research.
Crucially, determining how to rationally structure the rubric input process to guide a model to more rapidly and efficiently probe the capability boundaries of its base architecture during RL continues to be a vital research frontier.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>LLM for Health</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The successful scaling of language models has spurred significant interest in their application to healthcare <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singhal et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singhal et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tanno et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thirunavukarasu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Prior work has demonstrated their potential across a range of targeted tasks, including generating differential diagnoses, assisting with clinical documentation, supporting mental health treatment, and drafting radiology reports <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">McDuff et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Oh et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As large language models become more deeply integrated into clinical workflows, the need for agentic architectures has become critical.
The inherent complexity of healthcare—demanding multi-task reasoning and the use of diverse external tools—requires a more adaptive and interactive paradigm.
Consequently, LLM-driven agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ferber et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> have emerged as a foundational framework for translating these models into actionable intelligence within real-world medical environments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.15859v1/ORBIT_pipeline.png" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/347;" width="476" height="347" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>The overall pipeline of ORBIT, a useful method to align LLMs on open-ended complex tasks via rubrics-based incremental training.
(a) Sampling medical consultation cases, truncating dialogues to form realistic queries, and generating distilled supervision data for SFT training.
(b) During the RL phase, we adopt the GRPO framework, where a dynamic, query-specific rubric is generated for each medical case.
Each response is evaluated by an external Judge model based on this rubric, and the resulting score serves as the reward signal for policy optimization.
(c) For each medical consultation case, a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) framework constructs a tailored, multi-dimensional rubric.
The retrieval module references HealthBench rubrics as exemplars, while the generation model is guided to avoid direct duplication.
These rubrics are used solely by the external Judge model for evaluation, ensuring that the agent remains unbiased and enabling stable policy exploration.
The RAG framework is also extensible, supporting integration of larger and more diverse medical consultation datasets.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>ORBIT</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problems setup</span>
Given a clinical case in realistic world, whether in chat format or in outpatient chart format, ORBIT will turn it into practical RL training data.
Through filter strategies from different levels, the final <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;dialogue,rubrics&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;dialogue,rubrics&gt;</annotation></semantics></math> pair is fed for RL training to optimize the policy gradient of the base LLM.
Basically it follows three steps: <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Dialogue QA simulation ‣ 3 ORBIT ‣ InfiMed-ORBIT: Aligning LLMs on Open-Ended Complex Tasks via Rubric-Based Incremental Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> Dialogue QA simulation; <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Rubrics Generator with In-Context Learning ‣ 3 ORBIT ‣ InfiMed-ORBIT: Aligning LLMs on Open-Ended Complex Tasks via Rubric-Based Incremental Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> Rubrics Generator with In-Context Learning; <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Rubrics-Based Reinforcement Learning ‣ 3 ORBIT ‣ InfiMed-ORBIT: Aligning LLMs on Open-Ended Complex Tasks via Rubric-Based Incremental Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> Rubrics-Based Reinforcement Learning.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Dialogue QA simulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Whether starts from chat format or in outpatient chart format, we can prompt LLM to generate a feasible multi-turn dialogue.
The agentic synthesis methods are practical ways to produce multi-turn dialogue data.
For simplicity, we finish the main experiments using the testset dialogue data from DoctorAgent-RL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
After we prepare the dialogue QA data, we utilize the in-context learning ability of advanced LLMs to generate rubrics step by step.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Rubrics Generator with In-Context Learning</h3>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Diagnostic Database Construction</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a seed dataset <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\{(q_{i},\mathcal{R}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\{(q_{i},\mathcal{R}_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> derived from the HealthBench rubrics and an embedding-based LLM <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{emb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{emb}</annotation></semantics></math>, the goal is to construct a semantically enriched diagnostic database.
Here, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the textual dialogue history of the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th medical consultation case, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{i}=\{r_{i,1},r_{i,2},\dots,r_{i,n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{i}=\{r_{i,1},r_{i,2},\dots,r_{i,n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the corresponding set of diagnostic rubrics.
Each dialogue <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and rubric <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> is first transformed into its semantic representation through <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{emb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{emb}</annotation></semantics></math>, producing embedding vectors <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{q_{i}}=\mathcal{M}_{emb}(q_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{q_{i}}=\mathcal{M}_{emb}(q_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{r_{i,j}}=\mathcal{M}_{emb}(r_{i,j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{r_{i,j}}=\mathcal{M}_{emb}(r_{i,j})</annotation></semantics></math>.
These embeddings are then stored in a structured vector database to support efficient semantic retrieval and matching.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Two distinct data pools are then constructed:
<span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(i)</span> The <em id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">case–rubric pair pool</em> (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{cr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{cr}</annotation></semantics></math>), which consists of all case–rubric pairs, formally defined as</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{cr}=\{(q_{i},\mathcal{R}_{i},\mathbf{e}_{q_{i}},\sum_{r\in\mathcal{R}_{i}}\mathbf{e}_{r})\mid(q_{i},\mathcal{R}_{i})\in D\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{cr}=\{(q_{i},\mathcal{R}_{i},\mathbf{e}_{q_{i}},\sum_{r\in\mathcal{R}_{i}}\mathbf{e}_{r})\mid(q_{i},\mathcal{R}_{i})\in D\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(ii)</span> The <em id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rubric pool</em> (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>), which collects all unique rubrics and their embeddings across the dataset, defined as</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{r}=\{(r,\mathbf{e}_{r})\mid r\in\bigcup_{(q_{i},\mathcal{R}_{i})\in D}\mathcal{R}_{i}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{r}=\{(r,\mathbf{e}_{r})\mid r\in\bigcup_{(q_{i},\mathcal{R}_{i})\in D}\mathcal{R}_{i}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This yields a semantically aligned diagnostic database, facilitating fine-grained retrieval, similarity reasoning, and embedding-based analysis of case–rubric relationships.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Diagnostic Candidates Searching</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a new query <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, representing the dialogue history of a patient’s medical consultation, we first obtain its semantic embedding <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{q}=\mathcal{M}_{emb}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{q}=\mathcal{M}_{emb}(q)</annotation></semantics></math> using an embedding model <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{emb}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{emb}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then we compute the similarity between <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and all embeddings stored in our diagnostic database, which comprises a case–rubric pair pool (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{cr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{cr}</annotation></semantics></math>) and a rubric pool (<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the similarity scores, the top-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{cases}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>cases</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{cases}}</annotation></semantics></math> most relevant cases are retrieved from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{cr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{cr}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the top-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{rubrics}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>rubrics</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{rubrics}}</annotation></semantics></math> initial candidate rubrics are selected from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Subsequently, a reranker model <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{re}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{re}</annotation></semantics></math> is employed to refine the ranking of these initial candidates, balancing computational efficiency with matching accuracy.
Through this two-stage retrieval and reranking process, we obtain the set of top-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{cases}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>cases</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{cases}}</annotation></semantics></math> relevant cases, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the set of top-<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{\text{rubrics}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mtext>rubrics</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{\text{rubrics}}</annotation></semantics></math> semantically aligned rubrics, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3 </span>Rubrics Generation Process</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The retrieved cases <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and rubrics <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> serve as in-context input for the generation process.
We employ a generative model, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, and provide it with a prompt constructed from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, and task-specific instructions.
This guides the model to produce <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> rubric candidates for the query <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}(q)=\{r_{1},\dots,r_{m_{g}}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}(q)=\{r_{1},\dots,r_{m_{g}}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This generated set of rubrics serves as a checklist for evaluating the performance of a model’s response to the input medical query.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.4 </span>Difficulty filter with Pass@k</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To improve the quality of our training data and enhance reinforcement learning efficiency, we introduce a two-stage filtering strategy.
First, we filter the input cases to select for intermediate difficulty. This step isolates a productive learning zone by removing problems that are either too trivial to be informative or too complex to be solvable.
Second, we filter the generated rubrics, retaining only those that set a sufficiently high performance standard. This is critical for creating a strong optimization gradient, as overly lenient rubrics fail to drive meaningful model improvement.
These filters can be designed to ensure that the model trains on solvable yet challenging problems, guided by rigorous evaluative criteria, thereby maximizing the efficiency of the RL process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This process begins with a scoring step.
For each query <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, we prompt the current model <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{rollout}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>rollout</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{rollout}}</annotation></semantics></math> responses, denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Y}_{q}=\{y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{n_{\text{rollout}}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>rollout</mtext></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Y}_{q}=\{y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{n_{\text{rollout}}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A judge model then evaluates each response <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> against the set of generated rubrics <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This yields a satisfaction score <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(y_{i},r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(y_{i},r)</annotation></semantics></math> for each response-rubric pair.
Based on these scores, we apply two distinct filtering methods in parallel.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sample-Level Filtering: Selecting Moderately Difficult Cases.</span>
This filter aims to identify queries that are at an appropriate difficulty level for the model. We first calculate an average score for each sample <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> by averaging the satisfaction scores across all its rollouts and rubrics:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{s}_{q}=\frac{1}{n_{\text{rollout}}\cdot|\mathcal{S}_{q}|}\sum_{i=1}^{n_{\text{rollout}}}\sum_{r\in\mathcal{R}_{q}}S(y_{i},r)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>rollout</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>rollout</mtext></msub></munderover><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{s}_{q}=\frac{1}{n_{\text{rollout}}\cdot|\mathcal{S}_{q}|}\sum_{i=1}^{n_{\text{rollout}}}\sum_{r\in\mathcal{R}_{q}}S(y_{i},r),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of candidate positive scores for this query.
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{s}_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{s}_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> reflects the model’s overall performance on the query <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
A high score suggests the case is too easy, while a low score indicates it is too hard.
We then apply a dual-threshold filter to retain only the moderately challenging samples:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\text{filtered}}=\{q\mid\tau_{q}^{\text{low}}\leq\bar{s}_{q}\leq\tau_{q}^{\text{high}}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mtext>filtered</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>q</mi><mtext>low</mtext></msubsup><mo>≤</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>s</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>q</mi><mtext>high</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\text{filtered}}=\{q\mid\tau_{q}^{\text{low}}\leq\bar{s}_{q}\leq\tau_{q}^{\text{high}}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">By keeping only the samples whose scores fall within the range <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\tau_{q}^{\text{low}},\tau_{q}^{\text{high}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>q</mi><mtext>low</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>τ</mi><mi>q</mi><mtext>high</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\tau_{q}^{\text{low}},\tau_{q}^{\text{high}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, we focus the training process on the most instructive examples where the model has room to improve.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rubric-Level Filtering: Refining Rubric Quality</span>
This filter is designed to discard rubrics that are too simple and fail to challenge the model.
For each rubric <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, we calculate its <span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pass rate</span>, defined as the proportion of rollouts that satisfy the rubric’s criteria.
We consider a rubric <span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">passed</span> if its satisfaction score exceeds a threshold <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., 0.8).
The pass rate for rubric <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> across the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{rollout}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>rollout</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{rollout}}</annotation></semantics></math> responses for a query <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P(r,q)=\frac{1}{n_{\text{rollout}}}\sum_{i=1}^{n_{\text{rollout}}}\mathbf{1}\{S(y_{i},r)\geq\tau_{s}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>rollout</mtext></msub></mfrac><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>rollout</mtext></msub></munderover><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(r,q)=\frac{1}{n_{\text{rollout}}}\sum_{i=1}^{n_{\text{rollout}}}\mathbf{1}\{S(y_{i},r)\geq\tau_{s}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{1}\{\cdot\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{1}\{\cdot\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function.
Rubrics with a very high pass rate are considered too easy and provide little value for reinforcement learning. We filter them out using a pass rate threshold <math id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}_{q,\text{filtered}}=\{r\in\mathcal{R}_{q}\mid P(r,q)&lt;\tau_{r}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>filtered</mtext></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}_{q,\text{filtered}}=\{r\in\mathcal{R}_{q}\mid P(r,q)&lt;\tau_{r}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">This ensures that the retained rubrics are relatively challenging to guide the model’s optimization effectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Ultimately, this filtering strategy enhances the quality of both the training samples and their corresponding rubrics, yielding a more robust and efficient dataset for the subsequent reinforcement learning stage.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Rubrics-Based Reinforcement Learning</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is a memory-efficient variant of PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite> that avoids a value network by using a group baseline.
For each query <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, we can produce <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> rollouts from the original policy model <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{old}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{old}</annotation></semantics></math> and we need to optimize the following tagert function</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E8X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E8Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{q,\{o_{i}\}}\left[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{|o_{i}|}\sum_{t=1}^{|o_{i}|}P_{t}(\theta)-\beta D_{\text{KL}}[\pi_{\theta}\|\pi_{\text{ref}}]\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>KL</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{q,\{o_{i}\}}\left[\frac{1}{G}\sum_{i=1}^{G}\frac{1}{|o_{i}|}\sum_{t=1}^{|o_{i}|}P_{t}(\theta)-\beta D_{\text{KL}}[\pi_{\theta}\|\pi_{\text{ref}}]\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the clipped <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> can be presented as</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{t}(\theta)=\min\left(r_{t}(\theta)\hat{A}_{i,t},\text{clip}(r_{t}(\theta),1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon)\hat{A}_{i,t}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>clip</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{t}(\theta)=\min\left(r_{t}(\theta)\hat{A}_{i,t},\text{clip}(r_{t}(\theta),1-\varepsilon,1+\varepsilon)\hat{A}_{i,t}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where the importance weighting token-level ratio and the group-normalized advantage are</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}(\theta)=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i,t}|q,o_{i,&lt;t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(o_{i,t}|q,o_{i,&lt;t})},\hat{A}_{i,t}=\frac{R(q,o_{i})-\bar{R}_{G}}{\sigma_{G}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}(\theta)=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(o_{i,t}|q,o_{i,&lt;t})}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(o_{i,t}|q,o_{i,&lt;t})},\hat{A}_{i,t}=\frac{R(q,o_{i})-\bar{R}_{G}}{\sigma_{G}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{R}_{G}=\frac{1}{G}\sum_{j=1}^{G}R(q,o_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>G</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{R}_{G}=\frac{1}{G}\sum_{j=1}^{G}R(q,o_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> is the group mean reward and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> its standard deviation and the KL term discourages large policy drift from the reference model <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While the GRPO framework is a highly unified and simplified representation for reinforcement learning training of LLMs.
However, designing appropriate reward functions for open-ended scenarios remains a challenging problem.
Conventional reward function design predominantly relies on rule-based matching—for instance, extracting the answer from the final boxed segment in a response and assigning a binary reward <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> based on its correctness, after which advantages are defined via group-wise comparisons.
Such rule-based paradigms face significant difficulties in scaling to open-ended benchmarks.
Moreover, an oversimplified reward function can lead to training instability and generate uninformative feedback.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, we propose a reward framework that treats rubrics as dynamic, semantic scoring rules.
As detailed in Sec <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Rubrics Generator with In-Context Learning ‣ 3 ORBIT ‣ InfiMed-ORBIT: Aligning LLMs on Open-Ended Complex Tasks via Rubric-Based Incremental Training" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, we use a RAG system as rubrics generator model, denoted as <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{rubrics}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{rubrics}</annotation></semantics></math>, to produce a tailored set of rubrics for each input query <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{rubrics}(q,\text{task})\rightarrow\{r_{1},r_{2},\dots,r_{n}\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>task</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{rubrics}(q,\text{task})\rightarrow\{r_{1},r_{2},\dots,r_{n}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Each rubric <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}=\{\text{criterion}_{i},\text{point}_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mtext>criterion</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>point</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}=\{\text{criterion}_{i},\text{point}_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a specific evaluation criterion and its corresponding importance score.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To calculate the reward for a given response, we employ a separate large language model (LLM) as a <span id="S3.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Judge Model</span>.
This judge evaluates the responses (<math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) generated by the current policy <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\text{old}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\text{old}}</annotation></semantics></math> against each criterion.
The overall reward for a query <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and response <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the sum of points from all satisfied criteria, computed as:</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E12X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R(q,o_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R(q,o_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{j=1}^{n}\text{match}(q,o_{i},r_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mtext>match</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{j=1}^{n}\text{match}(q,o_{i},r_{j})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E12Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{j=1}^{n}\text{match}(q,o_{i},\text{criterion}_{j})\times\text{point}_{j}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mtext>match</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>criterion</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mtext>point</mtext><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{j=1}^{n}\text{match}(q,o_{i},\text{criterion}_{j})\times\text{point}_{j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">Here, the Judge Model executes the <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{match}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>match</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{match}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> function, producing a binary output (1 if the criterion is satisfied, 0 otherwise).
Each response thereby accumulates points based on the number of criteria it fulfills.
This aggregated score forms the rollout reward, which in turn is utilized to calculate the advantage function <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}_{i,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}_{i,t}</annotation></semantics></math> and construct the policy update for our rubric-based reinforcement learning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Related Settings</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Medicine Dialogue Datasets.</span>
Our primary analyses were performed on a focused corpus of 2,082 test samples, meticulously curated from three prominent Chinese medical benchmarks: IMCS21 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, CHIP-MDCFNPC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and MedDG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. This dataset was designed to cover a diverse range of classic, multi-dimensional medical consultation scenarios, which is also the test dataset of doctorAgent-RL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmark.</span> HealthBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arora et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, an open-ended medical benchmark from OpenAI, comprises 5,000 full consultation cases.
It includes HealthBench Hard, a particularly challenging subset of 1,000 cases designed to test the limits of even state-of-the-art general-purpose models.
We therefore focus our core experiments on this demanding benchmark (Healthbench Hard) to provide a rigorous validation of our approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> The capabilities of smaller language models on complex, open-ended tasks are often underestimated.
To challenge this assumption, we select Qwen3-4B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>)</cite> as our base model.
We then design a comprehensive suite of experiments to systematically investigate its performance limits and highlight its potential.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hardware settings.</span> To ensure a fair comparison, we maintain a consistent batch size across all methods within each task.
All experiments were conducted on a cluster of 8 NVIDIA H800 GPUs, each with 80 GB of memory.
Four GPUs were allocated for training the primary models, while the remaining four were dedicated to serving the evaluation model with generated rubrics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Quantitative Results</h3>

